  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Production of standardised doors and windows for low-cost housing</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Twenty-seven years of war have had a tremendous impact on the rural population of Angola. A large number of people have fled into large towns like Luanda. After the war, a lot of people want to return to their home lands, but houses are completely demolished, caused by the war and by lack of maintenance. This is why the government of Angola announced a low-interest housing fund to be able to build houses again in the rural areas.Habitec Commercio and Industria S.A. is a social enterprise that makes school furniture from Eucalyptus wood in Huambo (central region). Together with their South African partner Cheswood Timbers cc, they have developed plans to start up a joint venture and invest in a joinery, dedicated to the production of standard size doors, door frames and windows. As they use Eucalyptus wood, they target the low-cost housing market. In the beginning they can deliver to a low-cost housing project established by the NGO who has founded Habitec Commercio and Industria S.A..This project will produce products that will be very useful to rural lower and middle class people and create a large amount of jobs outside the metropolis of Luanda or other coastal towns.</narrative>
  </description>
